WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.961
when we first started
looking into the Kowloon Walled City,

00:00:02.961 --> 00:00:07.298
also known as the densest city
in the world, we thought for a place

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:10.385
that's essentially a slum full of crime,

00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:13.596
drugs with subhuman living conditions.

00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:17.017
There sure is a lot of romanticization
about this place

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:21.938
In Japan, they even built a giant arcade
that mimics parts of Kowloon.

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.273
And I get it.

00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:26.818
The city looks like it came straight
out of a dystopian sci fi novel.

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:31.281
It's a giant megastructure part
architecture, part living organism,

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:36.202
and it's actually something architects
have been dreaming about for years.

00:00:36.286 --> 00:00:39.039
One continuous structure
where you could access

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:43.835
all the necessities of daily life
but evolves and grows with time.

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:48.339
But there's also stories after stories
from people who's lived there

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:51.968
or visited there that claim
to have fond memories of this place,

00:00:52.135 --> 00:00:55.013
describing it as an energetic, industrious

00:00:55.013 --> 00:01:00.060
patchwork of chaos
with a strange, compelling beauty.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:01.561
Beauty?

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:04.189
How could this possibly be beautiful?

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:06.566
So Kowloon walled City.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:15.200
Was there something more to this
than just chaos?

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:18.119
A portion of this video sponsored by Milanote

00:01:18.119 --> 00:01:20.872
I just want to give you a sense
of how dense it was.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:26.127
Here's the density of L.A.,
New York, Hong Kong and Kowloon.

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:29.714
and it almost tripled in population
in just a few decades.

00:01:29.714 --> 00:01:31.883
So how did this even happen?

00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:32.634
Kowloon Walled

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:37.138
City was a city within Hong Kong
that was technically a part of China.

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:42.685
over its lifespan from a small Chinese
military force to this.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:47.148
It was used as a political pawn
in the conflict between China and Britain,

00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:50.777
where both parties were more interested
in exerting power

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:53.238
than actually governing
this piece of land.

00:01:53.238 --> 00:01:56.699
In the end, the tension became unsolvable,

00:01:56.699 --> 00:02:00.537
so both parties
decided to take a hands off approach.

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:05.125
It was left alone and without
any government control or police presence.

00:02:05.208 --> 00:02:10.797
It just grew and grew
almost like a living organism.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:11.756
Buildings were built

00:02:11.756 --> 00:02:15.635
cheaply and quickly to accommodate
the huge influx of migrants.

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:20.890
a massive number of Chinese refugees
flooded here after World War Two as it was

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:25.186
known as the only Chinese enclave that
the Hong Kong government couldn't touch.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:30.358
But after that, it included anyone
and everyone from gangs, criminals

00:02:30.358 --> 00:02:34.404
to doctors to entrepreneurs, people
trying to escape poverty

00:02:34.404 --> 00:02:38.158
or people trying to capitalize
on this unregulated haven

00:02:38.366 --> 00:02:42.412
crime naturally flourished there
with gangs, drugs, brothels.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:46.416
If you had an industrial business,
you could ignore the fire codes, the labor

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:50.962
codes or safety codes So you could produce
goods at a fraction of the cost.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.965
you could also sell things
that were banned anywhere

00:02:53.965 --> 00:02:57.802
else, like dog meat

00:02:57.886 --> 00:02:59.762
with unbeatable prices.

00:02:59.762 --> 00:03:03.683
Industry kind of thrived here
and lots of things made in Kowloon.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:09.189
Wall City made their way back to China,
Hong Kong, and sometimes even overseas.

00:03:09.272 --> 00:03:15.278
they were known especially
for their fish balls and dumplings.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.488
The more we learned about the city,

00:03:17.488 --> 00:03:21.326
there was this idea
that kept popping up the rhizome.

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:25.705
But it's kind of a complicated concept,
so I'll hand it over to Cindy,

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:40.011
our researcher in Hong Kong native.

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:40.845
All right.

00:03:40.845 --> 00:03:43.514
Let's try again to explain.

00:03:43.514 --> 00:04:16.547
We have our Italian filmmaker

00:04:16.631 --> 00:04:18.466
The first rule of the rhizome

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:23.221
is that it's multiple multiplicity
without a structure.

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:24.222
Let me explain.

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:27.225
So in a traditional city,
you have your city grid

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:30.937
and they get populated with buildings,
with functions that are determined

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.230
by your planning department.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:36.609
you have your circulation at the base
and that's how you get from here to here.

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:37.944
But in Kowloon,

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:42.282
buildings will get built, leaving
these small gaps for air and light.

00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:45.368
But very quickly
they get filled in with stairways,

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:49.038
which sometimes connect it to three
or four buildings.

00:04:49.205 --> 00:04:53.835
The city of Kowloon had around
350 buildings, but eventually

00:04:53.835 --> 00:04:57.755
the all merge into this one
giant megastructure.

00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:01.301
The rooftops would connect,
forming one giant rooftop,

00:05:01.551 --> 00:05:04.929
and even the residential units
were connected to each other.

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:08.766
And since not all the units
had electricity or other resources,

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:12.395
it allowed them to share things
like power out of a single source

00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:17.775
It especially allow businesses
to expand strategically and organically.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.902
Let's look here, for example.

00:05:19.902 --> 00:05:23.573
This is a strip club,
but the real money was in gambling.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:26.367
So they connected these buildings
to lure people

00:05:26.367 --> 00:05:30.204
in with the strip club
and eventually bring them down here

00:05:30.288 --> 00:05:34.709
so new buildings could attach
and be integrated to existing structures.

00:05:34.917 --> 00:05:39.297
And with every new building,
new circulation paths and collection

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:43.384
points are formed which evolve and expand
with the growth of the city

00:05:43.593 --> 00:05:46.888
and at the intersection
of these connections or stairs

00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:49.891
or alleys, nodes would organically merge.

00:05:49.932 --> 00:05:52.810
Here we see a small convenience store
that serves

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:56.814
the people living on the upper story
so they don't have to go all the way down.

00:05:56.898 --> 00:06:02.236
The city only had a few roads but created
some semblance of order over here.

00:06:02.278 --> 00:06:06.366
And here we have the commercial areas
because of their direct connection

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:07.367
to the outside.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.952
It was full of shops,
clinics and factories

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:12.205
that naturally gathered on the ground
level.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:16.751
Chinese doctors and dentists
who couldn't afford to get relicensed in

00:06:16.751 --> 00:06:21.506
Hong Kong, set up shop here
and offer services for bargain prices,

00:06:21.589 --> 00:06:24.467
which attracted customers
from outside the city.

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:27.762
residential units sat on the levels above,
and this created

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:31.224
some of the most attractive
units in the city thanks to the daylight.

00:06:31.307 --> 00:06:34.894
street was called
Kwong Ming Street, directly translating

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:37.105
into light and bright.

00:06:37.105 --> 00:06:41.025
because it would be lit up at night
as drug addicts would gather here.

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:44.237
Both of these areas had lots of factories
because you could get

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:47.448
vehicle access here and water access here.

00:06:47.532 --> 00:06:50.660
So food factories
naturally gathered in this area.

00:06:50.868 --> 00:06:54.497
but most of the residents actually moved
through the hundreds of alleys

00:06:54.497 --> 00:06:58.084
and secret paths
which all twisted and turned

00:06:58.084 --> 00:07:02.296
and stepped up and down
and cut through multiple buildings.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:05.174
so unlike a typical city where you have

00:07:05.174 --> 00:07:09.220
one point of connection,
you had multiple points of connection

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:13.015
vertically and horizontally
between multiple spaces.

00:07:13.224 --> 00:07:17.061
In fact, it would be impossible to map
the entire network of the city

00:07:17.061 --> 00:07:21.232
using conventional mapping methods.

00:07:21.315 --> 00:07:21.816
If you've been

00:07:21.816 --> 00:07:24.944
following this channel,
you probably know that we use M.A.

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:28.573
to organize all of our research
from the early brainstorming

00:07:28.573 --> 00:07:31.576
session all the way to the final draft
of the research.

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:35.913
Just having a visual board clarifies
so many things

00:07:35.913 --> 00:07:39.917
and it allows us to make connections
that we didn't really see before.

00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:44.422
some of our team is remote,
so being able to share and edit the boards

00:07:44.422 --> 00:07:49.260
real time during our meetings
makes our collaboration very seamless.

00:07:49.427 --> 00:07:52.096
It's kind of replaced PDFs for us.

00:07:52.096 --> 00:07:55.183
My friends and I even started
planning our trip to Japan on M.A.

00:07:55.183 --> 00:07:57.477
because you can easily drop in your map,

00:07:57.477 --> 00:08:00.897
which saves a lot of hassle
when you're planning with more people.

00:08:01.105 --> 00:08:04.817
It's completely free with no time limits,
so you can sign up

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:06.736
using the link in the description.

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:10.656
So if you can't map it,
and without traditional landmarks

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:15.203
or views,
how do they navigate a city like this?

00:08:15.286 --> 00:08:17.997
Kowloon Generic Romance

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:35.556
set in Kowloon Walled City.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.851
Living here means
you need to create your own unique mental

00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:51.822
map that's tailored
to your personal experiences or routines.

00:08:51.906 --> 00:08:55.117
it's interesting
because Deleuze and Guattari also argue

00:08:55.117 --> 00:08:59.205
that a map should never be
a static representation because that fixes

00:08:59.205 --> 00:09:00.790
your thinking about a certain place.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:03.876
When your idea of a thing is fluid
and evolving,

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:07.213
you can be much more creative
in how you use that thing.

00:09:07.296 --> 00:09:10.800
So in that sense,
the city becomes not just a backdrop,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.803
but an active participant
in shaping the memories

00:09:13.803 --> 00:09:25.064
and identities of the residents.

00:09:25.147 --> 00:09:26.774
but unlike a rhizome,

00:09:26.774 --> 00:09:29.777
the Kowloon Walled City has a boundary,

00:09:29.777 --> 00:09:32.363
a very abrupt boundary.

00:09:32.363 --> 00:09:35.741
So Kowloon Walled City
started as a military fort

00:09:35.741 --> 00:09:39.537
with an actual wall around it,
and And that's where the name came from.

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:44.083
They built this fort after Britain
occupied Hong Kong as a way to create

00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:48.170
a visible Chinese military presence
near the new British colony.

00:09:48.254 --> 00:09:49.589
But during World War Two,

00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:53.259
Japan had taken over swaths of China,
including Hong Kong.

00:09:53.342 --> 00:09:56.679
And when they needed building materials
to expand the nearby airport,

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:59.515
They took the walls of Kowloon Wall City.

00:09:59.515 --> 00:10:03.102
And when Chinese refugees
flooded to Hong Kong after the war,

00:10:03.311 --> 00:10:06.480
They instinctively gravitated
towards the Wall City.

00:10:06.564 --> 00:10:10.234
And even without the physical wall,
the new buildings were built

00:10:10.401 --> 00:10:14.780
almost the same exact footprint
of the original walls,

00:10:14.864 --> 00:10:18.200
forcing it to expand upwards and inwards.

00:10:18.284 --> 00:10:19.702
It's probably also

00:10:19.702 --> 00:10:23.789
one of the conditions that forced it
to become such an interconnected city,

00:10:23.873 --> 00:10:28.502
but also what made it deteriorate
so quickly over time.

00:10:28.586 --> 00:10:32.214
This was the only place in the city
where you could see the sky

00:10:32.214 --> 00:10:33.424
and get some fresh air.

00:10:33.424 --> 00:10:36.677
And also the social heart
of the community.

00:10:36.761 --> 00:10:40.014
in fact, even though they didn't
have a government, the residents

00:10:40.056 --> 00:10:44.310
self-organized to fix problems
as they came up to deal with crime.

00:10:44.352 --> 00:10:47.396
They formed groups of volunteers
to escort single women.

00:10:47.647 --> 00:10:51.442
And when the Hong Kong government
released plans to demolish the city,

00:10:51.525 --> 00:10:54.570
they organized the Kowloon City
Anti Demolition Committee

00:10:54.737 --> 00:10:57.031
that fought against the plan for years.

00:10:57.031 --> 00:11:00.159
even the five Triad gangs organize garbage
cleaning

00:11:00.159 --> 00:11:03.371
teams and help settle disputes
between businesses.

00:11:03.454 --> 00:11:06.957
The courtyard was built around a yard
in the office

00:11:06.957 --> 00:11:10.836
building from the Military Forge,
built back in 1845.

00:11:10.878 --> 00:11:14.215
The building went through multiple
hands from the Christian Evangelist

00:11:14.215 --> 00:11:18.177
Commission, where they held schools
to the all people center.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:23.057
But it was preserved and the courtyard
as a whole became so essential to the

00:11:23.057 --> 00:11:27.269
community that the community restricted
any new construction in this area.

00:11:27.353 --> 00:11:31.065
And I think the preservation of this
courtyard is kind of fascinating.

00:11:31.232 --> 00:11:35.319
It speaks to our need for content,
unity and stability,

00:11:35.403 --> 00:11:38.823
even in a ever evolving chaos
like Kowloon Walled City.

00:11:39.031 --> 00:11:43.828
It speaks to our natural tendency
to gravitate around any kind of link

00:11:43.828 --> 00:11:56.340
to the past and how these physical anchors
can become the bedrock of a community.

00:11:56.424 --> 00:11:59.009
the common
buildings or all the remains of the city.

00:11:59.009 --> 00:12:03.222
Now, in 1994,
Carolyn Wall City was finally demolished.

00:12:03.305 --> 00:12:07.935
And what was once
a chaotic, pulsating hive of human life

00:12:08.018 --> 00:12:11.063
has been replaced
with a peaceful Chinese garden.

00:12:11.272 --> 00:12:16.360
I'm sure it was necessary, but
I can't help but feel a kind of emptiness.

00:12:16.569 --> 00:12:22.116
This raw, gritty, energetic,
urban anomaly has been sanitized,

00:12:22.199 --> 00:12:27.663
erased from this world existing
only in the minds of those who live there.

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:30.791
But quickly,
it's fading into distant memories,

00:12:30.791 --> 00:12:34.086
into hazy dreams, or even nightmares.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:38.507
But there is also a belief
that the essence of the place,

00:12:38.716 --> 00:12:43.804
its soul,
can transcend a physical dimension.

00:12:43.888 --> 00:12:47.767
So maybe the spirit of Kowloon Wall City

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:55.191
still lingers on.

00:12:55.274 --> 00:12:55.608
There were

00:12:55.608 --> 00:13:00.196
some really fantastic books
that we were friends for this research.

00:13:00.279 --> 00:13:02.031
Some were analytical,

00:13:02.031 --> 00:13:07.286
some were kind of fictional,
and some were very R rated. R.

00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:08.412
In case you're interested in

00:13:08.412 --> 00:13:11.457
any of these,
I will leave the links in the description.

00:13:11.582 --> 00:13:13.209
We also did a lot of research

00:13:13.209 --> 00:13:16.921
for this video, a lot of which
we had to translate from Chinese.

00:13:17.004 --> 00:13:20.341
So if you're interested in our research
booklet, we will also leave that

00:13:20.341 --> 00:13:21.717
in the description.

00:13:21.717 --> 00:13:23.969
We also have a newsletter
and a Discord server.

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:25.805
If you want to stay updated
with what we're doing

00:13:25.805 --> 00:13:28.808
What do you guys think about this
crazy city?

00:13:29.016 --> 00:13:30.351
Let us know in the comments.

